  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe goal is to find a step function h:[0,2]->[0,1] minimizing the maximum overlap with its shifts, i.e., minimizing C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx. We will use a hybrid gradient-free search method: a genetic algorithm for baseline finding coupled with local gradient-based optimization to refine the solution. This approach addresses local-optima avoidance while maintaining feasibility of constraints. Key limitations of earlier methods:\n\u2022 Prior local optimizers may get stuck in suboptimal regions\n\u2022 Increasing n_points exponentially expands search space\n\u2022 Objective evaluation is computationally expensive\n\nThe central idea: combine global population diversity with iterative constraint projection via smooth approximation. We use:\n- Genetic operators (mutation/crossover) to explore solution space\n- Smooth objective surrogates to avoid discrete constraints\n- Fast gradient-free optimization variants for scalability\n- Progressive resolution refinement (100\u2192400\u21921000 points)\n\nThis beats the previous method by:\n\u2022 Easier implementation of projection via soft constraints\n\u2022 Reduced need for frequent recomputation at different resolutions\n\u2022 Better exploration-exploitation balance\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nVariables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h_i = n/2, x in [0,2]\nConstraints: 0\u2264h\u22641 and integral constraint\nObjective: min max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\u2022 Discretized via linear correlation (including dx factor)\n\u2022 Smoothed using Legendre-Fenchel transform for gradient-based methods\n\u2022 Projection incorporated through penalty terms\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nProject feasible projection:\n```python\ndef project_feasible(h, n, target_sum=0.5):\n    h_clipped = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n    current_sum = sum(h_clipped) / n\n    if abs(current_sum - target_sum) < 1e-7:\n        return h_clipped\n    # Iterative volume smoothing\n    for _ in range(1000):\n        diff = target_sum - current_sum\n        room = np.clip(1 - h_clipped, 0, 1) if diff < 0 else np.clip(0 - h_clipped, -1, 0)\n        if abs(sum(room) - (-diff)) < 1e-7:\n            return h_clipped\n        h_clipped += (10 * diff * room) / (sum(room) + 1e-12)\n        h_clipped = np.clip(h_clipped, 0, 1)\n    return h_clipped\n```\n\nSurrogate objective:\n```python\ndef smooth_obj(x, n, alpha, dx=2.0/n):\n    c = np.correlate(x, 1.0-x, mode='full')\n    center = n-1\n    c_centered = c[center:]  # Drop out-of-bound lags\n    m = c_centered.max()\n    shifted = alpha*(c_centered - m)\n    mx = shifted.max()\n    if abs(mx) < 1e-12:\n        lse = 0.0\n    else:\n        unexp = np.log(np.sum(np.exp(shifted-mx)))\n        lse = mx + unexp\n    return (m + lse/alpha) * dx\n```\n\nCore hybrid optimization:\n\n```python\ndef run_evolution(n_points, max_evals=1000):\n    # Gaussian mutation\n    mutation = 0.05 + 0.1*random.random()\n    # Blended crossover operation (twopoint crossover)\n    def crossover(a, b):\n        j = random.randint(0, n_points)\n        k = random.randint(j, n_points)\n        return np.concatenate([a[:j], b[j:k], a[k:]])\n    \n    # Base objective including smoothness penalty\n    def actual_obj(h):\n        return compute_c5(h, n_points) + 10*max(0, min(project_feasible(h, n_points)[sum(h)/n_points-0.5], 10))\n    \n    # Local optimization function\n    def local_optimize(h):\n        # Use scaled SLSQP with increased tolerance\n        return slsqp_opt(h, n_points, [10, 100, 1000])\n\n    # Evolution loop\n    pop_size = 50\n    h_pop = [project_feasible(np.random.rand(n_points), n_points) for _ in range(pop_size)]\n    evals = 0\n    best_h = min(h_pop, key=actual_obj)\n    best_c = actual_obj(best_h)\n    \n    # Iterative evolution-supervised loop\n    while evals < max_evals and time_left(residual_time):\n        next_pop = []\n        # Tournament selection with elitism\n        sorted_pop = sorted(zip([actual_obj(h) for h in h_pop], h_pop), key=lambda x: x[0])\n        next_pop.extend([sorted_pop[i][1] for i in range(5)])  # Elitism top 5\n        for _ in range(pop_size-5):\n            # Selection\n            a = random.choice(sorted_pop[:pop_size//2][1])\n            b = random.choice([x for (_,x) in zip([actual_obj(x) for x in h_pop], h_pop)])\n            # Crossover\n            child = crossover(a, b)\n            # Polynomial mutation\n            child += (np.random.rand(n_points) - 0.5) * mutation * 0.3\n            child = project_feasible(child, n_points)\n            next_pop.append(child)\n        h_pop = next_pop\n        current_best = min((actual_obj(h), h) for h in h_pop)\n        if current_best[0] < best_c:\n            best_h, best_c = current_best\n        evals += len(h_pop) // 2  # Estimate half mutations per generation\n    \n    # Final refinement\n    best_h = local_optimize(best_h)\n    return best_h, best_c, n_points\n```\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n\u2022 Base parameters:\n  \u03c3 = 0.05 (mutation rate)\n  Pop size = 50 (reducible by 20% without performance)\n  Max evals = 1000\n\u2022 Key hyperparameters:\n  Mutation rate \u03c3\n  \u03b7 (crossover rate)\n  Tolerance tol=1e-5\n\u2022 Useful variations:\n  Adaptive mutation: Increase \u03c3 during initial exploration\n  Progressive resolution: Gradually increase n_points\n  Different population sizes for varying n_points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\u2022 Dominant costs: Correlation (size proportional to n^2)\n\u2022 Timings:\n  n=100: ~2s/eval\n  n=400: ~9s/eval\n  n=1000: ~36s/eval\n\u2022 Schedule:\n  Phase1 (1000 evals): n=100(pop_size=10) \u03f5=0.02, budget=400s\n  Phase2 (2000 evals): n=400(pop_size=20) \u03f5=0.005, budget=600s\n  Phase3 (1000 evals): n=1000(pop_size=10) \u03f5=2e-3, budget=500s\n\u2022 Early termination:\n  If best_c < 0.3808, keep best solution\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Feasibility check: 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2 (enforced via projection)\n- Objectives: C_5 is the authoritative max_overlap\n- Intermediate evaluation: Requires all lags computation\n- Final fallback: Hierarchical re-projection ensures validity\n- Sanity checks:\n  None (satisfied by math)\n  \nThe algorithm minimizes C_5 via hybrid evolution and local optimization while ensuring constraints. This improves continuity of solution across scales.",
